Human: Can you tell me the steps to write an if/then statement in C programming language?

Assistant: Sure, here's a simple one I can teach you:

If you want to execute code if a certain condition is true, then you use an “if” statement.  For example:

```
int x = 5;

if ((x > 0) && (x < 10)) {
  cout << "x is between 0 and 10" << endl;
}
```

This code will print out "x is between 0 and 10" because the condition `x > 0` and `x < 10` are both true.

If you want to execute code if a certain condition is false, then you use a “elseif” statement.  For example:

```
int x = 0;

if (x > 0) {
  cout << "x is positive" << endl;
} else if (x < 0) {
  cout << "x is negative" << endl;
} else {
  cout << "x is zero" << endl;
}
```

This code will print out "x is zero" because the condition `x > 0` is false, but the condition `x < 0` is true.  And the condition `x < 0` is false, but the condition `x > 0` is true.  So the code in the second if statement gets executed.

If you want to execute code if a certain condition is false, then you use a “else if” statement.  For example:

```
int x = -1;

if (x > 0) {
  cout << "x is positive" << endl;
} else if (x < 0) {
  cout << "x is negative" << endl;
} else if (x == 0) {
  cout << "x is zero" << endl;
} else {
  cout << "x is not zero" << endl;
}
```

This code will print out "x is positive" because the condition `x > 0` is false, but the condition `x < 0` is true.  And the condition `x < 0` is true, but the condition `x > 0` is false.  So the code in the third if statement gets executed.

If you want to execute code if a certain condition is false, then you use an “else” statement.  For example:

```
int x = 0;

if (x > 0) {
  cout << "x is positive" << endl;
} else if (x < 0) {
  cout << "x is negative" << endl;
} else if (x == 0) {
  cout << "x is zero" << endl;
} else {
  cout << "x is not zero" << endl;
}
```

This code will print out "x is not zero" because the condition `x > 0` and `x < 0` are both false, and the condition `x == 0` is true.  So the code in the last if statement gets executed.

If you want to execute code if a certain condition is false, then you use an “end if” statement.  For example:

```
int x = 0;

if (x > 0) {
  cout << "x is positive" << endl;
} else if (x < 0) {
  cout << "x is negative" << endl;
} else if (x == 0) {
  cout << "x is zero" << endl;
} else {
  cout << "x is not zero" << endl;
}

if (x > 0 && x < 10) {
  cout << "x is between 0 and 10" << endl;
}
```

This code will print out "x is not zero" because the condition `x > 0` and `x < 10` are both false, and the code in the second if statement gets executed.

